  "bible": {
    "title": "Range 01 — first playable modules",
    "intent": "Modern industrial collection bay, booth console and turning target in one continuous concourse. Select the collection bay, walk to the console, start/stop the target and reset it. A separate prototype game extension provides a first-person firearm and animated hands with shots/hits counters. Prototype materials and environment, not final hero art. The intended larger campus retains varied configurable lanes and the chronological collection wall; this one-booth slice is a milestone, not a reduction of scope.",
    "rules": "Stable placement and joint IDs own interaction. Source assets may change appearance without recreating gameplay. Maker is the Holodeck walkthrough inhabitant. Current built-in runtime supports joint/relay target interaction; prototype firing and animated hands are provided by game code, not these built-in modules. Aggregate hit counters are not full zone scoring or shot review.\n\nPlanned scope, not implemented: paper/precision, reactive plates, moving targets, timed/arcade sequences, long field and configurable practice profiles. The six-bank/48-station layout remains an exploratory option, not a fixed count; multiple activities can reuse a compatible lane. Retain the existing activity research and visual/reference direction.\n\nTarget-game design requirement: an authoritative versioned session/string/shot event ledger records accepted shots and resolved scoring hits, non-scoring hits or misses. Store target/surface identity, activation and scoring revisions, shot/query-time transform snapshots, target-local impacts, surface UV and zone results. Cosmetic feedback never independently awards points. Numbered marks follow the current target for display; historical shot snapshots remain immutable. The console and world target share that ledger, with per-string and session results.\n\nProposed paper-target loop: preview, deploy, ready, active string, end, return, docked review, then redeploy/retry or finish. End string and return blocks accepted fire during travel/review, clears held input and preserves results. Carrier failures stop with explicit recovery; new sheets archive the old result and receive new identity. Other target types use compatible reset/electronic review instead of assuming physical retrieval. All targets are abstract game surfaces; scoring values, motion, lane counts and difficulty remain versioned design decisions. See docs/range-targets-and-shot-review.md for the proposed contract and acceptance checks.\n\nPer-firearm fidelity requirement, not implemented: resolve behavior from an exact weapon/configuration/ammunition profile, never era alone. Version accuracy/dispersion, projectile simulation, handling, weapon motion, moving parts, camera response and effects separately. Give each value field-level provenance: reference-backed, estimated or gameplay-tuned. Recoil tendencies and bounded seeded variation are distinct from a fixed authored spray pattern. Do not label the generic pistol prototype or its placeholder recoil/flash as historically verified.\n\nOne accepted/resolved shot event drives muzzle effects, smoke, weapon/hands motion, camera feedback, audio, surface-local hit marks and material-specific impact effects. Store query-time target transforms, impact point/normal and surface identity; rendering effects never recalculate score or authoritative ballistics. Validate obstruction, moving targets, first-person projection, event timing and browser/native replay before fidelity claims. See docs/shot-feedback-fidelity.md. User reports hub and PC under load; current timing/FPS measurements cannot qualify performance."
  },
